{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,10,16]],"date-time":"2026-10-16T06:13:13Z","timestamp":1792131193818,"version":"4.4.0"},"reference-count":41,"publisher":"American Economic Association","issue":"11","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["American Economic Review"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2020,11,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The credibility revolution in economics has promoted causal identification using randomized control trials (RCT), difference-in-differences (DID), instrumental variables (IV) and regression discontinuity design (RDD). Applying multiple approaches to over 21,000 hypothesis tests published in 25 leading economics journals, we find that the extent of p-hacking and publication bias varies greatly by method. IV (and to a lesser extent DID) are particularly problematic. We find no evidence that (i) papers published in the Top 5 journals are different to others; (ii) the journal \u201crevise and resubmit\u201d process mitigates the problem; (iii) things are improving through time. (JEL A14, C12, C52)<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1257\/aer.20190687","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2020,10,28]],"date-time":"2020-10-28T08:05:43Z","timestamp":1603872343000},"page":"3634-3660","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":291,"title":["Methods Matter: p-Hacking and Publication Bias in Causal Analysis in Economics"],"prefix":"10.1257","volume":"110","author":[{"given":"Abel","family":"Brodeur","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Economics, University of Ottawa (email: )"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nikolai","family":"Cook","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Economics, University of Ottawa (email: )"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Anthony","family":"Heyes","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Departme